From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: a translated line is never lost - shown late or in
 its own words

A local model can translate more slowly than dense dialogue is spoken.
A cue whose translation came back after its start was still added with
its own start and stop, so it showed only for what was left of its
window, or not at all. A request that failed delivered empty text, so
its lines vanished, and the worker then waited ten seconds before the
next request.

A failed request is now asked again at once, unless the cues it is for
are all over. A request that still fails, or was not asked again
because its cues were over, makes the next one wait the ten seconds as
before; a request a seek dropped does not. The lines of a request that
failed are not kept as context for the next, so a request asked again
does not see them twice.

When the request still fails, each cue of it is delivered as it came -
its own text, repaired if the repair is on - instead of empty, and a
cue the model's answer left out, or only partly translated, is shown in
its own words too. Each such request logs one warning with the number
of lines shown untranslated. A cue with nothing to translate in it (only
tags) is still delivered empty, to end the cue before it.

A translated line of a stream delivered after its start is shown from
the next picture, up for at least two seconds or to its own stop if
that is later; on a paused picture - playback waiting for the
translation - it appears as playback goes on, not over the still.
"Now" is the playback time moved by the subtitle delay, which is the
time libass is asked to render at: the video's time minus the player's
subtitle delay. The worker is given the same time, so a stream's
request is not asked again for cues already over either; a cue with no
end of its own, which the next one ends, counts as over from its start.
A stream's cues still go to the model in the order they came: moved
behind newer ones, a late line would come long after its end, or after
the line that follows it. Only a file, known ahead of playback, has its
cues still to come go before those playback is past. A line more than
ten seconds after its end is left at its own times: it belongs to
another scene by then.

Late lines are chained rather than piled up: a late line ends the late
line before it at its own start, but no sooner than two seconds after
that one was shown, so at most two are on screen at once. Every other
place that ends the line before - a clear in a picture track, the next
line of a text track at the same place, an empty cue, an open-ended
line - also keeps a late line up those two seconds. A picture track
still ends a line by the next one's own start, compared with this
one's own start rather than where it was shown from, so a late line is
not left up for the twenty seconds an unended picture gets.

A subtitle file's lines are not moved: the file is translated ahead of
playback from where it is, and a moved line would keep its moved times
for good and show at the wrong moment after a seek back. Nor is it done
for a disc, a TV channel or a recording, whose playback time counts
from a start of their own rather than the streams' time stamps; a
stream from an input stream add-on that reports its own start time
(VideoPlayer's GetTimes path) has the same mismatch, cannot be told
from here, and gets no late handling in effect - "now" is behind every
line. So does a video with EDL cuts past its first cut: the player's
time leaves out what is cut and the streams' time stamps do not, so the
late handling, and the check for cues already over, quietly stop there.

The cues the worker drops - by a seek, while waiting or during their
request, or because the subtitle closes, whether in a request or still
queued - are now logged, as is a line come too late to be shown and a
picture read ahead before a seek. The worker reaches nothing of Kodi's,
so it logs through the chat, which gains a Note() the provider's chat
writes to the log.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

